# SURU Custom Rules — DNS-over-HTTPS (DoH) resolver-bypass detection
# MITRE ATT&CK: TA0005 Defense Evasion / T1572 Protocol Tunneling;
#               TA0011 Command and Control / T1071.001 Web Protocols
#
# SID block: 9990000–9999999 (SURU project-local range; never overlaps ET Open)
#
# Each rule MUST be on a single physical line — Suricata requires it.
# Validation: suricata -T -S suru-doh-bypass.rules
#   (or on the router: sudo suricata -T -c <iface suricata.yaml> -S /tmp/suru-doh-bypass.rules)
#
# This is the INLINE leg of DoH detection; suru-doh-detect.zeek is the passive
# ssl.log leg (two engines, defense-in-depth). A client resolving names over
# HTTPS bypasses the local unbound + pfBlockerNG DNSBL enforcement entirely.
#
# Router upstream-DoH exemption: if the pfSense resolver itself is configured to
# forward over DoH, add the router's WAN/egress IP to the Suricata Pass List so the router's
# own upstream DoH does not alert — do NOT hardcode an exception here.
#
# FP note: Firefox and iOS/macOS enable DoH by default, so this fires on benign
# hosts too — it is a policy-visibility control as much as a threat detector.
# The tls.sni alternation is the published DoH endpoint hostname set; keep it in
# sync with suru-doh-detect.zeek's doh_endpoints.

# ── Rule 9990003 — DoH endpoint SNI from an internal client (TA0005/T1572, TA0011/T1071.001) ──
alert tls $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"SURU DoH Resolver Bypass - Known DoH endpoint SNI (TA0005/T1572, TA0011/T1071.001)"; flow:established,to_server; tls.sni; pcre:"/^(?:cloudflare-dns\.com|mozilla\.cloudflare-dns\.com|chrome\.cloudflare-dns\.com|one\.one\.one\.one|dns\.google|dns\.quad9\.net|doh\.opendns\.com|dns\.nextdns\.io|doh\.cleanbrowsing\.org|dns\.adguard-dns\.com)$/i"; classtype:policy-violation; sid:9990003; rev:1; metadata:tactic TA0005, technique T1572, affected_product Any, deployment Perimeter, falsepositives Firefox_iOS_macOS_enable_DoH_by_default_and_router_upstream_DoH;)
